                    "comment": "scipy.coherence - which rhythms two signals share"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 862,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1066,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Measures, for each frequency, how consistently two signals move together at that rhythm: 1 where they share it in a steady relationship, 0 where they do not. It does not care whether one leads the other - only whether the relationship holds.\n\nWhere a single correlation mixes all rhythms together, coherence separates them: two dancers may share the slow sway of the music while their fast gestures are independent;\na breathing rhythm may lock to a movement while everything else drifts.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.coherence <window: int> <sample rate>\nDefaults: window 64, sample rate 60.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\na:\nThe first signal. Every input is compared and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nb:\nThe second signal, held: send it first. Only as much of the two as they both cover is used.\n\nsample rate (float box, also an inlet):\nSamples per second, so frequencies come out in cycles per second.\n\nwindow (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many samples per window; the signals are cut into overlapping windows and averaged. Longer gives finer frequency steps (sample rate / window apart) but fewer windows to average, so a noisier estimate. Coherence needs several windows to mean anything.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncoherence:\nFrom 0 to 1 at each frequency.\n\nfrequencies:\nThe frequency of each value. Sent first.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe make_two_rhythms subpatcher makes a (3 and 7 cycles a second) and b (3, shifted in time, and 11). Their coherence is near 1 at 3 cycles a second - they share it, even though shifted - and low elsewhere, including at 7 and 11, which each has alone.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.correlate finds the lag between two signals. scipy.welch gives each signal's own spectrum. np.corrcoef compares two signals as a whole.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 1050,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Measures, for each frequency, how consistently two signals move together at that rhythm: 1 where they share it in a steady relationship, 0 where they do not. It does not care whether one leads the other - only whether the relationship holds.\n\nWhere a single correlation mixes all rhythms together, coherence separates them: two dancers may share the slow sway of the music while their fast gestures are independent;\na breathing rhythm may lock to a movement while everything else drifts.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.coherence <window: int> <sample rate>\nDefaults: window 64, sample rate 60.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\na:\nThe first signal. Every input is compared and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nb:\nThe second signal, held: send it first. Only as much of the two as they both cover is used.\n\nsample rate (float box, also an inlet):\nSamples per second, so frequencies come out in cycles per second.\n\nwindow (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many samples per window; the signals are cut into overlapping windows and averaged. Longer gives finer frequency steps (sample rate / window apart) but fewer windows to average, so a noisier estimate. Coherence needs several windows to mean anything.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncoherence:\nFrom 0 to 1 at each frequency.\n\nfrequencies:\nThe frequency of each value. Sent first.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe make_two_rhythms subpatcher makes a (3 and 7 cycles a second) and b (3, shifted in time, and 11). Their coherence is near 1 at 3 cycles a second - they share it, even though shifted - and low elsewhere, including at 7 and 11, which each has alone.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.correlate finds the lag between two signals. scipy.welch gives each signal's own spectrum. np.corrcoef compares two signals as a whole."
                },
